WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.482
This slide makes row multiplication concrete using Alice in the practice data.

00:00:04.482 --> 00:00:09.267
Alice has two admissions, two lab tests, and two prescriptions.

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:13.612
These are six real source records across three child tables.

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:18.292
But if we join all three child tables independently by patient ID,

00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:22.677
each admission can combine with each lab result and each prescription.

00:00:22.677 --> 00:00:27.707
Two admissions times two labs times two prescriptions produces eight joined combinations.

00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:35.382
The table on the slide shows the eight possible admission-lab-prescription combinations.

00:00:35.382 --> 00:00:37.237
The SQL underneath measures this effect.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.662
COUNT star tells us how many joined combinations exist.

00:00:40.662 --> 00:00:46.672
COUNT DISTINCT admission ID tells us how many unique admissions exist.

00:00:46.672 --> 00:00:49.512
The same idea is used for lab test IDs and prescription IDs.

00:00:49.512 --> 00:00:54.452
Run the query and compare joined rows with the distinct event counts.

00:00:54.452 --> 00:00:58.707
This is an important debugging technique.

00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:01.542
If joined rows are much larger than the number of real events,

00:01:01.542 --> 00:01:06.342
the query may be combining several one-to-many relationships.

00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:12.952
The rows are not necessarily duplicate rows in the technical sense.

00:01:12.952 --> 00:01:13.452
They may be unique combinations.

00:01:13.452 --> 00:01:20.862
The analytical problem is that those combinations can inflate measures if we count them as if each row

00:01:20.862 --> 00:01:22.364
were a new event.
